  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">XXX029N Strengthening Community Capacity 3 (implementation)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description promeva:title="Description (English)" promeva:type="104" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CARE Nederland and CARE Yemen, together with consortium partners, RNW Media and SOS Center for Youth Capabilities Development (SOS), formed a Consortium&#x2019;, to implement the program titled &#x2018;Women, Peace, and Security (WPS) in Yemen. The overall aim of this program is to improve women&#x2019;s meaningful influence in relief, recovery and peace processes in Yemen, in Taiz and Eden. The program will contribute to all three sub-objectives of the Dutch WPS grant instrument, including strengthening the protection for women, addressing harmful social norms, and increasing opportunities for women&#x2019;s meaningful participation in relief, recovery, and peacebuilding, as well as supporting the priorities of the Netherlands Cooperation in Yemen. The program also supports the implementation of sustainable development goals (SDGs) 5 and 16, focusing on gender equality and on peaceful and inclusive societies, respectively.  
The goals of the NAP are particularly relevant for Yemen&#x2019;s ongoing, multi-track peace process. To varying degrees, the peace process engages women via Track 1, which involves official diplomatic efforts and formal negotiations; Track 2, which involves consultations with civil society organizations (CSOs). And Track 3, which refers to community-level initiatives for peacebuilding and conflict prevention. The Consortium&#x2019;s WPS programming aims to strengthen the involvement of women in the different tracks of the peace process (with more emphasis on tracks 2 and 3) and women&#x2019;s role in relief and recovery. Moreover, it aims to strengthen women&#x2019;s protection from SGBV and influence social norms that constrain or undermine women&#x2019;s participation and protection to build a more supportive environment for women&#x2019;s inclusion in peace and security.

The TOC is built around three sub-objectives and four domains of change.
Sub-Objective 1: More equitable social norms and related practices
Sub-Objective 2: Enhanced protection for women
Sub- Objective 3: More inclusive relief, recovery, and peace processes

Domain of Change 1: Civil society is capable, representative, engaged in collective action
Domain of Change 2: Male awareness and support to women&#x2019;s contributions and leadership
Domain of Change 3: Powerholders are more supportive to women
Domain of Change 4: Women are empowered to influence relief/recovery/peacebuilding</narrative>
  </description>
